type: software
title: "Learning to aggregate Bayesian forecasts: optimal sample complexity under bounded dependence"
abstract: "Unrefereed research candidate. Several experts report calibrated probabilities for a yes/no event; an aggregator learns how to combine them from T past cases with known outcomes. In the binary model of Lin and Chen (NeurIPS 2023), with n experts and at most m signal values each, the minimax excess Brier risk is of order min{1, log(1/delta)/T + min(eta, m^n/T)} for every bound eta on the conditional total correlation of the reports, up to universal constants, and one estimator attains it without knowing eta. So about log(1/delta)/epsilon cases are needed under conditional independence and about (m^n + log(1/delta))/epsilon under arbitrary dependence, raising the earlier m^(n-2) lower bound to m^n. Also: the rate with a known prior, unbounded report alphabets, and finitely many outcomes."
